The system is defined by \[y(t)=\max\left(0,x(t)\right).\] <strong>Linearity:</strong><br/>Consider the input \[x(t)=1.\] Then, \[T\{x(t)\}=1.\] For the scaled input, \[-\,x(t)=-1,\] we get \[T\{-x(t)\}
=\max(0,-1)
=0.\] However, \[-\,T\{x(t)\}
=-1\neq0.\] Hence, the homogeneity property is violated. Therefore, the system is <strong>non-linear</strong>. <strong>Time Invariance:</strong><br/>Let the input be delayed by \(t_{0}\): \[x_{1}(t)=x(t-t_{0}).\] The corresponding output is \[\begin{aligned}
y_{1}(t)
&amp;=\max\!\left(0,x(t-t_{0})\right).
\end{aligned}\] Now delay the original output: \[\begin{aligned}
y(t-t_{0})
&amp;=\max\!\left(0,x(t-t_{0})\right).
\end{aligned}\] Since \[y_{1}(t)=y(t-t_{0}),\] the system is <strong>time-invariant</strong>. \[\boxed{\text{(d) Non-linear and time-invariant.}}\]

Given, \[\frac{dF(\omega)}{d\omega}=-\omega F(\omega), \qquad F(0)=1.\] Separating variables, \[\frac{dF}{F}=-\omega\,d\omega.\] Integrating, \[\ln F(\omega)=-\frac{\omega^2}{2}+C.\] Using \(F(0)=1\), \[C=0,\] hence \[F(\omega)=e^{-\omega^2/2}.\] Using the inverse Fourier transform, \[f(0)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}F(\omega)\,d\omega
=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-\omega^2/2}\,d\omega.\] Since \[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-\omega^2/2}\,d\omega=\sqrt{2\pi},\] we obtain \[f(0)=\frac{\sqrt{2\pi}}{2\pi}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}
\approx0.399&lt;1.\] Therefore, \[\boxed{\text{(a) }f(0)&lt;1}\]

Using the standard Z-transform pair, \[a^n u[n]
\overset{\mathcal{Z}}{\longleftrightarrow}
\frac{z}{z-a}.\] Differentiating both sides with respect to \(a\), \[n a^{n-1}u[n]
\overset{\mathcal{Z}}{\longleftrightarrow}
\frac{z}{(z-a)^2}.\] Multiplying both sides by \(a\), \[n a^n u[n]
\overset{\mathcal{Z}}{\longleftrightarrow}
\frac{az}{(z-a)^2}.\] Now, \[\frac{z^2}{(z-a)^2}
=\frac{z}{a}\cdot\frac{az}{(z-a)^2}.\] Using the property \[a^n u[n]
\overset{\mathcal{Z}}{\longleftrightarrow}
\frac{z}{z-a}\] we get \[x[n]=\frac{1}{a}(n+1)a^{\,n+1}u[n]
=(n+1)a^n u[n].\] \[\boxed{\textbf{(b) $(n+1)a^n u[n]$}}\]

The given system is \[\dot{x}=-x+u\] \[y=x\] The control law is \[u=-Kx\] Substituting into the state equation, \[\dot{x}
=
-x-Kx\] \[\dot{x}
=
-(1+K)x\] Therefore, the closed-loop state matrix is \[A_{cl}=-(1+K)\] Hence, the closed-loop pole is \[s=-(1+K)\] Given that the closed-loop pole is located at \[s=-2\] Therefore, \[-(1+K)=-2\] \[1+K=2\] \[K=1\] \[\boxed{1}\]

Given, \[G_p(s)=\frac{14.4}{s(1+0.1s)}
\qquad
G_c(s)=1\] The open-loop transfer function is \[G(s)=G_p(s)G_c(s)=
\frac{14.4}{s(1+0.1s)}\] For unity feedback, \[\frac{C(s)}{R(s)}
=
\frac{G(s)}{1+G(s)}
=
\frac{\dfrac{14.4}{s(1+0.1s)}}{1+\dfrac{14.4}{s(1+0.1s)}}
=
\frac{144}{s^2+10s+144}\] Comparing the characteristic equation with the standard second-order form, \(s^2+2\zeta\omega_n s+\omega_n^2\) \[\omega_n^2=144 \quad =&gt; \omega_n=12~\text{rad/s}\] \[2\zeta\omega_n=10\] \[2\zeta(12)=10\] \[\zeta=\frac{5}{12}\] The damped natural frequency is \[\omega_d
=
\omega_n\sqrt{1-\zeta^2}
=
12\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{5}{12}\right)^2}
\approx 10.91~\text{rad/s}\] \[\boxed{10.91}\]

Magnetic force on a moving charge is given by the Lorentz force equation: \[\vec{F}
=
q(\vec{v}\times\vec{B})\] Given, \[q=1\,\text{C}\] \[\vec{v}
=
10\,\hat{x}\] \[\vec{B}
=
10y\,\hat{x}
+
10x\,\hat{y}
+
10\,\hat{z}\] Therefore, \[\vec{F}
=
1
\left[
10\hat{x}
\times
\left(
10y\,\hat{x}
+
10x\,\hat{y}
+
10\,\hat{z}
\right)
\right]\] Using \[\hat{x}\times\hat{x}=0,
\qquad
\hat{x}\times\hat{y}=\hat{z},
\qquad
\hat{x}\times\hat{z}=-\hat{y}\] we get \[\vec{F}
=
100x\,\hat{z}
-
100\,\hat{y}\] At \[x=0^{+}\] \[\vec{F}
=
-100\,\hat{y}\,\text{N}\] Hence, \[|\vec{F}|
=
100\,\text{N}\] \[\boxed{100}\]

Given, \[N_{\max}=650\,\text{rpm}, \qquad P=8,\qquad f=50\,\text{Hz}\] Synchronous speed is \[N_s=\frac{120f}{P}
=\frac{120\times 50}{8}
=750\,\text{rpm}\] Slip corresponding to maximum torque is \[s_m=\frac{N_s-N_{\max}}{N_s}
=\frac{750-650}{750}
=0.1333\] For maximum torque, \[s_m=\frac{R_2}{X_2}\] Hence, \[X_2=\frac{R_2}{s_m}
=\frac{0.08}{0.1333}
\approx 0.60\,\Omega\] \[R_2=0.08\,\Omega,\qquad X_2=0.60\,\Omega\] For maximum starting torque, \[R_2=X_2\] Therefore, \[R_2+R_{ext}=X_2\] \[R_{ext}=X_2-R_2\] \[R_{ext}=0.60-0.08
=0.52\,\Omega\] \[\boxed{0.52}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Slip at maximum torque is given by \(s_m=\dfrac{R_2}{X_2}\).</li><li>Maximum starting torque occurs when total rotor resistance equals rotor standstill reactance.</li><li>External rotor resistance is added in slip-ring induction motors to improve starting torque.</li></ul></div>

Given, \[P_{in}=40\,\text{kW}\] Stator losses \[P_{stator}=1\,\text{kW}\] Friction and windage losses \[P_{fw}=2.025\,\text{kW}\] Stator output (air-gap power): \[P_g=P_{in}-P_{stator}
=40-1
=39\,\text{kW}\] Slip: \[s=\frac{N_s-N_r}{N_s}
=\frac{1000-975}{1000}
=0.025\] Mechanical power developed: \[P_m=P_g(1-s)
=39(1-0.025)
=38.025\,\text{kW}\] Motor shaft output: \[P_{out}=P_m-P_{fw}
=38.025-2.025
=36\,\text{kW}\] Efficiency: \[\eta=\frac{P_{out}}{P_{in}}\times 100\] \[\eta=\frac{36}{40}\times 100
=90\%\] \[\boxed{90}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Air-gap power \(P_g=P_{in}-\text{stator losses}\).</li><li>Rotor copper loss equals \(sP_g\).</li><li>Mechanical power developed is \(P_m=P_g(1-s)\).</li><li>Shaft output power is obtained after subtracting friction and windage losses from \(P_m\).</li></ul></div>

For Newton-Raphson load flow in polar coordinates, \[\begin{bmatrix}
\Delta P \\
\Delta Q
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
H &amp; S \\
M &amp; R
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\Delta \delta \\
\Delta |V|
\end{bmatrix}\] The sub-matrix \(M\) relates reactive power mismatches \(\Delta Q\) with voltage angle corrections \(\Delta\delta\). Number of rows of \(M\): \[\text{Rows} = \text{Number of reactive power equations}\] Reactive power equations exist only for PQ buses. \[\therefore \text{Rows} = N_L\] Number of columns of \(M\): \[\text{Columns} = \text{Number of angle variables}\] Voltage angle variables exist for all buses except the slack bus. \[\therefore \text{Columns} = N-1\] Hence, the dimension of matrix \(M\) is \[\boxed{\text{(A)}\quad N_L\times(N-1)}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>In NR load flow using polar form, voltage magnitude corrections exist only for PQ buses.</li><li>Voltage angle corrections exist for all buses except the slack bus.</li><li>Matrix \(M\) represents sensitivity of reactive power with respect to voltage angle.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not include PV buses while counting reactive power equations.</li><li>Remember that matrix dimensions depend on unknown variables, not total buses alone.</li></ul></div>

For economic operation without transmission losses, \[IC_1=IC_2\] Therefore, \[40+0.2P_1
=
32+0.4P_2\] Rearranging, \[0.2P_1-0.4P_2=-8\] \[P_1-2P_2=-40
\tag{1}\] Also, total load demand is \[P_1+P_2=260
\tag{2}\] Solving equations (1) and (2), \[P_1=160\,\text{MW}\] \[P_2=100\,\text{MW}\] Hence, \[\boxed{
\text{(B)}\;
P_1=160,\;
P_2=100
}\]

The equivalent conductor resistance of the two cables connected in series is \[(R_{eq})_{cond}
=
0.7+0.5\] \[(R_{eq})_{cond}=1.2\,\Omega\] The insulation resistances are connected in parallel. <img src="img/e3a37be6b766c416.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,333px)"> Hence, \[\frac{1}{(R_{eq})_{in}}
=
\frac{1}{600\times10^6}
+
\frac{1}{900\times10^6}\] \[(R_{eq})_{in}
=
360\times10^6\,\Omega\] Required ratio is \[\frac{(R_{eq})_{in}}
{(R_{eq})_{cond}}
=
\frac{360\times10^6}{1.2}\] \[=
300\times10^6\] Therefore, \[\boxed{300}\]

The worst-case peak diode voltage occurs at maximum input voltage: \[(V_D)_{\text{peak}}
=
(230+20\%)\sqrt{2}
=
\left(230+230\times\frac{20}{100}\right)\sqrt{2}\] \[=
276\sqrt{2}
\approx 390.32\,\text{V}\] Hence, the answer is \[\boxed{390.32}\]

For a boost converter in CCM: \[V_0
=
\frac{V_S}{1-\alpha}
=
\frac{15}{1-0.6}
=
37.5\,\text{V}\] \[I_0
=
\frac{V_0}{R}
=
\frac{37.5}{10}
=
3.75\,\text{A}\] Using ideal power balance: \[I_S
=
\frac{I_0}{1-\alpha}
=
\frac{3.75}{0.4}
=
9.375\,\text{A}\] <strong>Verifying CCM operation</strong> For the boost converter, the inductor ripple current is: \[\Delta I_L
=
\frac{\alpha V_S}{fL}
=
\frac{0.6\times15}{25\times10^3\times1\times10^{-3}}
=
0.36\,\text{A}\] Minimum inductor current: \[I_{L,\min}
=
I_S-\frac{\Delta I_L}{2}
=
9.375-0.18
=
9.195\,\text{A}&gt;0\] Hence, the converter operates in CCM. Therefore, input resistance is: \[R_{\text{in}}
=
\frac{V_S}{I_S}
=
\frac{15}{9.375}
=
1.6\,\Omega\] \[\boxed{1.6}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For boost converter CCM: \(V_0=\dfrac{V_S}{1-\alpha}\).</li><li>Equivalent input resistance: \(R_{\text{in}}=(1-\alpha)^2R\).</li></ul></div>
